     label.marginnote-ind { display: inline; cursor: default; }
     ```

   - **`/Users/dhruva/src/dhruvasagar/website/assets/js/sidenote-math.js`** (NEW FILE)
     - Created to handle LaTeX rendering in sidenotes/marginnotes
     - The article uses backticks around LaTeX: `` `$\downarrow

 `` → Goldmark renders as `<code>$\downarrow$</code>` → KaTeX skips `<code>` elements
     - This script: strips `<code>` wrapping when content contains `

, then re-runs KaTeX on sidenote elements
     - Theme-agnostic: lives in site's own assets, not the theme
     ```javascript
     document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
       if (typeof renderMathInElement === 'undefined') return;
       document.querySelectorAll('.sidenote code, .marginnote code').forEach(function (code) {
         var text = code.textContent;
         if (/\$/.test(text)) {
           var span = document.createElement('span');
           span.textContent = text;
           code.parentNode.replaceChild(span, code);
         }
       });
       document.querySelectorAll('.sidenote, .marginnote').forEach(function (el) {
         renderMathInElement(el, {
           delimiters: [
             { left: '$', right: '$', display: true },
             { left: ', right: ', display: false },
             { left: '\\(', right: '\\)', display: false },
             { left: '\\[', right: '\\]', display: true }
           ]
         });
       });
     });
     ```

   - **`/Users/dhruva/src/dhruvasagar/website/layouts/partials/head/head.html`** (NEW FILE)
     - Overrides the theme's head partial to inject sidenote-math.js after the main bundle
     - This is the Hugo template override mechanism (site layouts take precedence over theme)
     - Exact copy of theme's head.html + one addition at the end:
     ```html
     <!-- Sidenote/marginnote math rendering (theme-agnostic) -->
     {{ $sidenoteJs := resources.Get "js/sidenote-math.js" | minify | fingerprint }}
     <script defer src="{{ $sidenoteJs.RelPermalink }}"></script>
     ```

4. Errors and Fixes:
   - **`markdownify` wrapping sidenote content in `<p>` tags**: Invalid HTML (`<p>` inside `<span>`) causes browser to break sidenote DOM → content leaks into regular flow. Fixed by using `$.Page.RenderString (dict "display" "inline")` instead.
   - **LaTeX `$\downarrow

 inside backticks renders as `<code>`**: The article source uses `` `$\downarrow

 `` with backticks. KaTeX skips `<code>` elements. Fixed by creating `sidenote-math.js` that unwraps code tags containing `

 before running KaTeX.
   - **Double-subtraction of 14rem in text max-width**: Used `max-width: min(calc(100% - 14rem), 38rem)` but `100%` already resolves to post's CONTENT area (which excludes the 14rem padding). So 14rem was subtracted twice, making text only 444px instead of ~700px. Fixed to just `max-width: 38rem`.
   - **Horizontal scrollbar at 1280px after removing content max-width**: `flex: 1 1 auto` with default `min-width: auto` prevents content from shrinking below its natural content width (1064px > 845px available). Fix: added `min-width: 0` to `.container.content` at ≥80em. This fix was just applied and NOT YET VERIFIED.

5. Problem Solving:
   - **Sidenote layout**: Solved by using `padding-right: 14rem` on `.post` (creates a right "column") and `margin-right: -14rem` on sidenotes (floats them into that padding column). This ensures text and sidenotes never compete for the same space.
   - **Content filling screen**: Solved by removing `max-width: 52rem` from `.container.content` (set to `none`). Content now fills available viewport width. Text elements are capped at `38rem` for readability.
   - **LaTeX in sidenotes**: Solved with a custom JS file that strips `<code>` wrapping and re-runs KaTeX on sidenote elements. Injected via a head.html partial override — theme-agnostic approach that persists across theme changes.
   - **Horizontal scroll at 1280px**: Root cause is flex's `min-width: auto` default. Fix (`min-width: 0`) just applied, not yet tested.

6. All User Messages:
   - "continue"
   - "The sidenote are looking better, but are still mixed up with the content, there's some empty space on the right that's left over, can we reclaim that and use the full screen better?"
   - [Mid-work message]: "Also the sidenotes supported latex enclosed within `$\downarrow

, this is from the previous theme, I would like to bring back this feature again in a generic way so even we switch themes it will persist. IMPORTANT: After completing your current task, you MUST address the user's message above. Do not ignore it."
   - [Mid-work message]: "I see a horizontal bar, and there's still a chunk of space on the right of the content that's wasted, can we reclaim that space? IMPORTANT: After completing your current task, you MUST address the user's message above. Do not ignore it."

7. Pending Tasks:
   - Verify that `min-width: 0` fix resolves the horizontal scrollbar at 1280px viewport
   - Check overall layout looks correct at both 1280px and 1920px after the fix
   - The "horizontal bar" the user mentioned — need to confirm what this refers to (was the horizontal scrollbar at bottom of page at 1280px which should now be fixed, or the thin vertical scrollbar indicator on the right)

8. Current Work:
   At 1280px viewport (exactly the 80em breakpoint), a horizontal scrollbar appeared after removing the `max-width: 52rem` cap on `.container.content`. Investigation revealed:
   - Content was 1064px wide at 1280px viewport (should be ~845px)
   - Content right was at 1459px (179px beyond viewport edge)
   - Root cause: flex item default `min-width: auto` prevents shrinking below content's natural width
   - The `post { padding-right: 14rem }` forces the post's natural size to be wide

   The fix just applied (not yet verified):
   ```css
   .container.content {
     max-width: none;
     min-width: 0;  /* ← JUST ADDED — allows flex shrink below content's natural size */
   }
   ```

9. Optional Next Step:
   Navigate to the page in the browser and take a screenshot at 1280px viewport to verify the `min-width: 0` fix resolved the horizontal scrollbar. Then check at 1440px and 1920px to confirm the full layout is correct at all breakpoints. The immediate pending work is verifying this fix:

   "At 1280px (exactly the 80em breakpoint): Content width: 1064px... hasHorizScroll: true... The simple fix: add min-width: 0 to .container.content which allows it to shrink below its content's natural width."

